# Session extracts: the agent session beside a lesson video

A **session extract** is the coding-agent session shown on screen in a lesson
video, as entries stamped with the video time they first appear
(`visibleFrom`). Matt produces them (repo `ai-hero-dev/session-extracts`); the
lesson page shows one beside the player and follows playback through it.

## Decisions

- **Name.** "Session extract", never a vendor name: the producer is a coding
  agent today and may be another tomorrow.
- **Where it lives.** `fields.sessionExtract` on the **video resource**, shaped
  `{ sessions: [...] }` (Matt's schema minus its `video` header; the video it
  sits on is the video it is timed against).
- **How it arrives.** Course sync only. `CourseJsonVideoV3.sessionExtract` in
  `packages/course-sync-schema` is optional, and `sessionExtract` is a
  sync-owned video field (`course-sync/resource-fields.ts`): a re-sync
  replaces it, a sync without it removes it.
- **Two validations, on purpose.** The sync contract checks the full shape and
  rejects the manifest, so a producer bug is loud at sync time. The lesson
  page parses it again in isolation (`parseSessionExtract`), so a bad stored
  value hides the panel and never breaks a lesson.
- **What the panel shows (Matt, 2026-10-30).** A skill run by slash command
  gets no row (the prompt that typed it already shows it), skill args are
  never shown, and the spoken block is marked by a gold bar in the left
  gutter, never by dimming the rest: at 2x the contrast change flickered.
- **One grid across tabs.** Live and the moment lists share it: the time
  left-aligned on its own line, content 12px in, the current-moment bar at
  4px. Hovering a moment lights its time with a play glyph; the time seeks.
  Rows sharing a time show it once, so the hover finds the head of the run.
- **Whole transcript up front (Matt, 2026-10-30).** Live lists every entry
  from the start so the transcript navigates the video: what the video has
  not reached sits at half opacity, following tracks the newest reached row
  (`data-current`), and a click on a user or assistant message plays from it.
  Tool and skill calls do not seek. The spoken-block match only searches
  replies the video has reached.
- **Following is ours, not `use-stick-to-bottom`.** With the whole
  transcript on the page the view follows the video's place, not the bottom.
  The library only escaped on a scroll up, so reading ahead never showed
  Follow video. Any wheel, touch, scrollbar or key scroll stops following;
  the button (arrow toward the video), a seek from the panel, or a seek back
  turns it on.

## Gotchas

- Matt's exporter omits an empty key instead of writing `null`, and leaves
  `skillBody` out to keep the manifest small (26 extracts made a 1.6 MB
  course.json). Both validations accept a missing key; the page reads it as
  `null`. Requiring the keys made the strict decoder reject the whole
  Cohort 005 manifest on 2026-10-29, so no extract reached prod.
- A rejected manifest logs only "Dropbox course manifest validation failed"
  (`lib/dropbox-course-sync.ts` drops the decode error). To find the field,
  read course.json from Dropbox and run the decoder on it.

- The course.json decoder runs with `onExcessProperty: "error"`. Any new field
  a producer adds fails the **whole** sync until the consumer schema accepts
  it, so the consumer change must deploy before the producer ships.
- Course sync stages the whole manifest in one request body (Vercel caps
  bodies at 4.5 MB). Extracts run 30-150 KB each; inline is fine for a cohort
  of solution videos, but a much larger course should move extracts to files
  referenced from the manifest.
- The "spoken block" highlight matches caption cues against the newest replies
  (`spokenBlockIndex`). It needs two shared word pairs and a clear winner, and
  only searches at or after the current reading anchor; looser matching
  produced false highlights while Matt riffed.
